40:This guide records a direction for lifting that limit without weakening the exact pipelines. The idea is a **tolerance contract** judged on what the program is for. For Candela that is the rendered frame. A pass would be accepted when the frames it produces stay within a measured bound of the original's, instead of when every call returns the same bits. The bound needs a principled source, and Candela supplies one. It is a Monte Carlo renderer, so its frames already differ from one random seed to the next, and that difference can be measured. A compile-time error that is small against the renderer's own noise is a defensible trade; one that is not, is not.
57:## 1. The compiler, and where exactness is enforced
69:Exactness is enforced at three places, and a tolerance contract has to say what happens at each:
73:3. **Per frame.** When a Candela revision is rendered, every output float is compared with the reference compiler's frame. IRWB-001 recorded zero differing floats for every comparison it made: B-01 at stride 4 from r0, from the O2 head and from this compiler's own image (diary steps 17 and 23), and all 18 per-scene programs at stride 8 (step 26). IRWB-001's diary calls rendering "a far stronger whole-program check than sampled function cases", because "bit-identical floats leave no room for tolerance arguments".
